The rule was attempting to detect security issues related to missing default cases in conditional statements (CWE-478). It had three parts: one for IF statements without else clauses, one for SWITCH statements without default cases, and one for unvalidated inputs in conditionals.

The specific error was a syntax error at line 32: `has_default = true {` because Rego doesn't allow multi-clause rule definitions with `=` assignment in this context. The syntax for defining rules with multiple cases uses separate rule bodies without the `=` operator.

To fix it, replace the problematic section with proper Rego rule syntax:
```rego
has_default {
    condition.is_default
}

has_default {
    condition.else_statement != null
    condition.else_statement.is_default
}
```
This defines `has_default` as a rule that's true when either condition matches, without using the `=` assignment syntax.

